{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Research output-path optimization. First isolate replacing std::endl with a newline and disabling unnecessary iostream synchronization before input. Then compare length-aware bulk writes against a reusable bounded output buffer, eliminating per-character formatted insertion and per-permutation flushing. Benchmark a few reasonable buffer sizes within the existing budget. Preserve exact output bytes, flush the final partial buffer, and support rows larger than the buffer without truncation or unchecked size arithmetic. Use one consistent output API. Keep enumeration changes limited to boundary safety so measurements identify output-path gains; provide G the best measured program and its evidence.\",\n  \"B\": \"Research permutation-generation and incremental formatting costs under a fixed buffered-output control. Compare boundary-safe std::next_permutation with a custom pivot/successor/reversal implementation that exposes the changed suffix. Maintain a reusable formatted row with punctuation and spaces initialized once; update only character positions affected by each transition, then append the complete row to the output buffer. Preserve native char ordering and duplicate handling without restricting alphabet or length. Avoid before-begin access on the final permutation and guard row-size arithmetic. Measure whether reduced formatting work beats its additional bookkeeping, and give G a separable kernel/formatting contribution rather than assuming it improves A.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research ordered multicore generation on the four-core ARM64 target, only using facilities supported by the unchanged build. Partition the multiset into disjoint lexicographically ordered prefix subtrees; enumerate each remaining suffix in sorted order without duplicate permutations. Send bounded output blocks through an ordered emitter, with backpressure and scheduling that cannot deadlock behind later subtrees. Never retain factorial-sized output or let workers interleave stdout. Compare serial execution with configurations using at most four active threads including the emitter. Use overflow-safe, input-derived work estimates for any serial/parallel threshold, not test identities. Account for repeated-symbol imbalance and thread startup: the short baseline and serial output stream may make parallelism unhelpful. Supply G both measured results and a correct serial fallback.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate only validated programs produced by A/B/C. Establish each candidate's unchanged-harness correctness and end-to-end timing, then test justified combinations within the fixed budget, prioritizing buffered output plus incremental formatting before adding parallelism. Measure every integration against its strongest constituent: gains are not additive, and copying, buffering or synchronization can cause regressions. Audit exact whitespace filtering, native char ordering, duplicate elimination, terminal iterator safety, empty and singleton behavior, output formatting, bounded storage and arithmetic overflow. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target and permissions unchanged. Retain the fastest reliably measured correct standalone or integrated program, including the baseline if necessary, and report the supporting evidence.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The active workload is Problem D, src/permutations.cc; A/B/C are research roles, not other contest problems. The only supplied measurement is a correct baseline across four cases: mean 0.135008 seconds, samples 0.133454–0.136196 seconds. There is no phase profile or candidate evidence. Per-character stream insertion and std::endl flushing every permutation make output handling the strongest source-derived hypothesis, not a measured bottleneck. Enumeration is already amortized efficient, while writing every required byte is unavoidable. Preserve removal of exactly the six listed whitespace characters, native char lexicographic ordering, distinct permutations, and rows consisting of '[ ', each character followed by a space, then ']\\\\n'. Empty filtered input produces no rows. The terminal suffix scan can decrement before begin; optimized candidates must use boundary-safe termination. Research should therefore prioritize buffered output, then incremental generation/formatting, with ordered parallelism treated as a higher-risk alternative.\"\n}"}}
